{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>El nahal eitan</b> — this is difficult, for it has never been worked, in the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 21:4). And our Sages said (Sotah 46a) that it is forbidden to plow and to sow in it forever. If so, they are prohibitions, so that one who sows or works it transgresses by this negative commandment, and the reason that they bring it out to nahal eitan is that it shall never be worked nor sown. And the reason for <b>and the priests, the sons of Levi, shall draw near</b> is to say: \"Atone for Your people Israel whom You redeemed\" (Deuteronomy 21:8), and the delimitation of the verses is: \"And the priests the sons of Levi shall draw near,\" and all the elders of that city who are nearest to the slain shall wash their hands, and they shall answer and say. And the washing applies to some of those mentioned and the declaration to some of them, in the manner of \"and you shall eat before YHWH your Elohim the tithe of your grain, your wine, and your oil, etc.\" (Deuteronomy 14:23). And regarding the matter of the breaking of the heifer's neck, R. Abraham ibn Ezra said (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 21:7) that HaShem commanded to do thus upon the nearby city, for had they not committed a transgression similar to it, it would not have happened that a person near them would be slain, and the thoughts of HaShem are deep without end as far as we are concerned. But the Rav said in the Guide of the Perplexed (III:40) that the reason is to expose the murderer and to eliminate his bloodguilt, for most of the time the murderer will be from the city in the vicinity of the slain, and when the elders go out and involve themselves in that measurement, and the elders of that city testify before the Creator that they were not negligent in maintaining and guarding the roads, and that they do not know who killed this person, and when the matter is investigated and the elders assemble and bring the heifer, many people will come to speak about it, and perhaps the matter will be revealed. And they already said (Jerusalem Talmudi, Sotah 9:1) that even if a maidservant comes and says, \"So-and-so is the murderer,\" the heifer's neck is not broken. And if the murderer is known and they keep silent about him and testify before the Creator on their lives that they did not know him, this constitutes great wantonness, and anyone who hears something about the matter will come and tell, and the matter will become publicized, and he will be killed either by a court or by the king or by the blood avenger. And the matter will be reinforced by the fact that the place where the heifer's neck is broken shall never be worked nor sown — those who see it will recognize it and speak about it. Now according to this reasoning there is benefit in this stratagem, but the act itself does not procure atonement on its own. And it would further have been fitting for it to be done in a good field suitable for sowing, so that those who see it would recognize it, for in nahal eitan it would not be known why it is not worked. And in my opinion there is in it a reason like the matter of the offerings performed outside: the scapegoat and the red heifer, and therefore the Sages counted the heifer whose neck is broken among the category of hukkim."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "Ramban cites the Guide of the Perplexed III:40; the reference (ג מ) abbreviates part 3, chapter 40."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:14:55.850348148Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}